<h2 class="sr-only">Qatar's American footprint — documented spending in U.S. universities, real estate, lobbying, and media, and how that connects to the jihad budget. Interactive cards expand details for each category.</h2>

<div class="sec-head">
  <div>
    <div class="eyebrow"><i class="ti ti-flag" style="font-size:11px" aria-hidden="true"></i> IJDB · Qatar influence · updated May 2026</div>
    <div class="title">Qatar's American footprint — and what it costs us to counter it</div>
    <div class="subtitle">Qatar funds Hamas. Qatar is also the largest foreign donor to American universities, owns Manhattan landmarks, and employs the most powerful lobbyists in Washington. Every dollar of U.S. counter-terrorism spending that targets Hamas, Hezbollah, and IRGC-linked networks is partly a response to Qatari-funded radicalization on American soil.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="alert-banner">
  <i class="ti ti-alert-triangle" style="font-size:15px;flex-shrink:0;margin-top:1px" aria-hidden="true"></i>
  <span><b style="color:var(--color-text-primary)">Important distinction.</b> The documented total of Qatar's U.S. spending is approximately $40–50 billion — not $400 billion. The $500 billion figure is Qatar's projected 10-year investment pledge, not yet spent. FORA tracks documented figures only. The numbers here are large enough on their own merits without inflation.</span>
</div>

