<!-- Detail panels -->
<div class="detail-panel" id="dp-univ">
  <div class="dp-head">
    <div><div class="dp-title">Universities — the soft power play</div><div class="dp-sub">Qatar is buying access to the American mind</div></div>
    <div><div class="dp-amount cr">$6.3B+</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);text-align:right">documented · DoE records</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-body">
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-danger)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>$1.2B in 2025 alone</b> — Qatar tripled its university funding year-over-year. Carnegie Mellon: $936M. Cornell Medical: $163M. Texas A&M: $24M. Northwestern has received $737M since 2008. Its contract with Qatar prohibits students and faculty from criticizing the Qatari government.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://freebeacon.substack.com/p/qatar-already-the-largest-foreign" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Washington Free Beacon · 2026</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:#BA7517"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>$980M between January 2023 and October 2024</b> — Qatar ramped up university donations after October 7, not despite it. As scrutiny increased, spending accelerated.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.meforum.org/mef-reports/america-for-sale-qatars-40-billion-spending-spree-buys-influence-and-control-of-elite-institutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Middle East Forum · July 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:#BA7517"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>Correlation with campus antisemitism.</b> Universities that have taken the most Qatari money have seen the greatest upticks in anti-Israel activity. Northwestern is the canonical case. No definitive causal link established — but correlative evidence is substantial.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.washingtonexaminer.com/in_focus/3916768/what-to-know-qatar-web-of-influence/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Washington Examiner · 2026</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>Middle East studies programs</b> — much of Qatar's university funding targets Middle East studies departments, many accused of promoting anti-Israel narratives. Qatar is the chief financial backer of the Muslim Brotherhood.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://thehill.com/opinion/international/5334791-qatars-us-influence-strategy-goes-much-further-than-you-think/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Hill · June 2025</a></div></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-response"><b>IJDB connection:</b> FBI post-10/7 enforcement surge — multiple Brooklyn, LA, Chicago, and Houston Hamas support networks arrested 2023–2024 — was partly a response to radicalization that investigators traced to campus environments. The FBI and DHS have both testified that university radicalization is a primary domestic recruitment vector for Palestinian extremism. Qatar-funded campuses are part of the threat landscape our counter-terrorism budget is built to monitor.</div>
</div>

<div class="detail-panel" id="dp-re">
  <div class="dp-head">
    <div><div class="dp-title">Real estate — owning the skyline</div><div class="dp-sub">Qatar Investment Authority · Qatari Diar</div></div>
    <div><div class="dp-amount ca">$6.2B+</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);text-align:right">Manhattan alone · since 2012</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-body">
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:#BA7517"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>Empire State Building and Plaza Hotel</b> — QIA owns stakes in two of New York's most iconic landmarks. Combined with other Manhattan holdings: $6.2B+ in NYC real estate since 2012.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.meforum.org/mef-reports/america-for-sale-qatars-40-billion-spending-spree-buys-influence-and-control-of-elite-institutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Middle East Forum · July 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>~$45B total U.S. investments</b> — QIA's total U.S. portfolio represents ~20% of its $524B in assets under management. Includes real estate, equities, private equity, and infrastructure.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.agbi.com/companies/qatar-investment-authority/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AGBI · QIA profile 2024</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>$500B 10-year pledge</b> — QIA has committed to investing $500B in the U.S. over the next decade. This is a strategic embedding, not passive investment — Qatar is buying leverage over American financial and political institutions.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://grokipedia.com/page/Qatar_Investment_Authority" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grokipedia · QIA profile 2025</a></div></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-response"><b>IJDB connection:</b> Real estate ownership is how Qatar embeds itself in American infrastructure. ICE and FBI counter-terrorism task forces have flagged Qatari Diar and QIA-linked entities in financial surveillance reviews. The IRGC and Hezbollah have used real estate investment networks as money-laundering channels — Qatar's ownership of American landmarks creates complexity in financial intelligence operations.</div>
</div>

<div class="detail-panel" id="dp-lobby">
  <div class="dp-head">
    <div><div class="dp-title">Lobbying and PR — buying Washington</div><div class="dp-sub">FARA filings · OpenSecrets · 2016–2024</div></div>
    <div><div class="dp-amount cb">$260M</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);text-align:right">8 years · 7th most of any foreign nation</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-body">
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-info)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>$8.4M annually on average 2016–2023</b> — Qatar has employed former U.S. Congress members and cabinet-level officials as lobbyists. Imaad Zuberi, paid ~$10M to secretly lobby the White House and Congress on Qatar's behalf, was sentenced to 12 years for failing to register as a foreign agent.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.meforum.org/mef-reports/america-for-sale-qatars-40-billion-spending-spree-buys-influence-and-control-of-elite-institutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Middle East Forum · July 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-info)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>2024 pivot to conservative media.</b> Since Trump's 2024 win, Qatar ramped up outreach to right-leaning outlets — over half its foreign-agent contacts now target conservative media, up from ~10% before. Qatar paid $180K/month to a firm that secured a Tucker Carlson interview for the Qatari prime minister.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.fischfiles.com/p/qatar-israel-fara-foreign-influence-93-billion-washington-dc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fisch Files · October 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>South Carolina political donations.</b> Zuberi donated to Sen. Lindsey Graham and SC Governor Henry McMaster's campaigns. Graham sits on the Senate Armed Services Committee — jurisdiction over the defense budget that funds counter-terrorism operations.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.meforum.org/mef-reports/america-for-sale-qatars-40-billion-spending-spree-buys-influence-and-control-of-elite-institutions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Middle East Forum · July 2025</a></div></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-response"><b>IJDB connection:</b> Qatar lobbies the same Congressional committees that set the counter-terrorism budget. Qatar also lobbies the executive branch that makes designations of foreign terrorist organizations. Hamas remains un-sanctioned as a Qatari government entity despite Qatar's documented financial support for Hamas leadership — this is not an accident.</div>
</div>

<div class="detail-panel" id="dp-media">
  <div class="dp-head">
    <div><div class="dp-title">Media and political influence</div><div class="dp-sub">Newsmax · Al Jazeera · Tucker Carlson · political donations</div></div>
    <div><div class="dp-amount ca">$230M+</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);text-align:right">documented · multiple channels</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-body">
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:#BA7517"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>~$50M into Newsmax</b> — A Qatari royal invested approximately $50 million in pro-Trump network Newsmax, leading senior newsroom staff to pressure reporters to soften coverage of Qatar.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.fischfiles.com/p/qatar-israel-fara-foreign-influence-93-billion-washington-dc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fisch Files · October 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:#BA7517"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>$180K/month to secure Tucker Carlson interview</b> — Qatar's PR apparatus paid a firm $180,000 per month specifically to arrange favorable media appearances, including an interview with the most-watched political commentator in the U.S.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.fischfiles.com/p/qatar-israel-fara-foreign-influence-93-billion-washington-dc" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fisch Files · October 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>Al Jazeera America</b> — Qatar launched and funded Al Jazeera America, a U.S.-based news network that operated 2013–2016. The network lost money but served as a diplomatic and influence operation. Al Jazeera remains active globally and has been the primary interview platform for Hamas leadership.</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-response"><b>IJDB connection:</b> A foreign government that funds Hamas is simultaneously buying favorable coverage from the most influential conservative media voices in America — during the same period that Hamas killed 46 Americans. This is the information-operations dimension of the jihad budget: the U.S. spends billions on counter-terrorism while one of Hamas's primary state sponsors buys the narrative.</div>
</div>

<div class="detail-panel" id="dp-qia">
  <div class="dp-head">
    <div><div class="dp-title">Qatar Investment Authority — total U.S. exposure</div><div class="dp-sub">Sovereign wealth fund · ~$524B AUM · est. 2005</div></div>
    <div><div class="dp-amount cb">~$45B</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);text-align:right">~20% of total AUM · current</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-body">
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-info)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>QIA does not publish its holdings.</b> The $45B U.S. figure is an estimate based on QIA statements (2019 target was $45B from $30B base) and Global SWF analysis that U.S. investments represent ~20% of AUM. Actual figure could be significantly higher.</div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-info)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>QIA ranks 10th globally</b> by deal activity in H1 2024, deploying ~$2B in that period alone. Its $524B in assets makes it one of the 10 largest sovereign wealth funds in the world — funded entirely by natural gas revenues from a nation of 330,000 citizens.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://www.agbi.com/companies/qatar-investment-authority/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AGBI · QIA profile 2024</a></div></div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-response"><b>IJDB connection:</b> OFAC (Treasury's sanctions office) and FinCEN (financial crimes enforcement) dedicate significant counter-terrorism budget to monitoring sovereign wealth fund transactions from state sponsors of terrorism. Qatar is a designated Major Non-NATO Ally — which legally constrains how aggressively the U.S. can sanction QIA activities even when those activities benefit Hamas.</div>
</div>

<div class="detail-panel" id="dp-proj">
  <div class="dp-head">
    <div><div class="dp-title">$500B pledge — the coming decade</div><div class="dp-sub">QIA 10-year U.S. investment commitment · 2025</div></div>
    <div><div class="dp-amount cp">$500B pledged</div><div style="font-size:10px;color:var(--color-text-tertiary);text-align:right">not yet spent · projected</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="dp-body">
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>Qatar has pledged to invest $500 billion in the U.S. over the next decade.</b> This commitment was made in 2025 as part of diplomatic engagement with the Trump administration — coinciding with U.S. negotiations over Hamas hostages and the Gaza ceasefire.<div class="dp-src"><a href="https://grokipedia.com/page/Qatar_Investment_Authority" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Grokipedia · QIA profile 2025</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="dp-row"><div class="dp-dot" style="background:var(--color-text-secondary)"></div><div class="dp-text"><b>This is leverage, not generosity.</b> A $500B investment commitment gives Qatar enormous diplomatic leverage over U.S. policy — including policy toward Hamas. The pledge was announced at the same time Qatar was mediating Hamas hostage negotiations, making the U.S. financially dependent on a Hamas patron.</div></div>
